WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:08.290
RODENTIA Director/Co-Writer Phoebe Jaspé:
I’ve been 24 countries, 4 continents and

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:11.780
I love Scandinavian culture.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:18.180
Something about me as a filmmaker, I grew
up in Abu Dhabi and I took my A-levels there

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.270
and I went to England for University.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:33.220
With RODENTIA this film I’ve directed it’s
about a CEO turning into a rat.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.570
So the idea came from mainly Franz Kafka’s
METAMORPHOSIS.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:46.260
My background stems from fine art so I used
to use a lot of oils and canvas and then this

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.780
developed into photography.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:57.819
Film is moving image, so every frame should
actually be like a painting.

00:00:57.819 --> 00:01:06.880
It’s very difficult to maintain this because
this takes a lot of time in creating shot

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.189
lists.

00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:17.330
We designed every single shot in the film
so it’s more to do with aesthetics and it’s

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.469
not a feminist film.

00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:30.579
The main character was actually based on myself
and of course I’m not that horrible but

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:34.190
it was a part of me that I wanted to destroy.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:42.899
The best way to express your negative traits
is though an art form and that’s the only

00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:56.020
way to have a release and of course to get
rid of depression or anxiety or manic psychosis

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:04.039
which is what my feature film is about, is
based on a 10-day manic psychosis episode,

00:02:04.039 --> 00:02:07.640
more on that later.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:14.300
So we wanted to explore in an authentic way
how does it feel becoming a rat.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:23.500
And losing your beauty is a huge theme in
the film and so is disfigurement.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:32.530
So if you combine disfigurement with traditional
beauty it creates a sort of duality in the

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:37.300
picture and I think that’s quite important
to explore.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:44.170
Actor Fabian Bolin:&nbsp; “I’m getting some
hair attached to my back by a special fx make-up

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:45.170
artist.”

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.090
Prop Maker and Special Makeup Effects Artist,
Jayne Hyman: “I’m just applying some hair,

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.230
this is crepe hair, it’s like a theatrical
hair that for special fx make-up.”

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:58.370
Actor Fabian Bolin:&nbsp; “I focus on more about
the character than the animal.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.330
I’m playing a rat, you know.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:06.620
And it takes quite a lot to get that realistically
with the movement and everything to focus

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:12.960
on and it helps not to bother and worry about
the mask or something falling off and stuff

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.090
like that.”

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:19.890
Adam Pearson has worked with Scarlett Johansson
in the film UNDER THE SKIN and UNDER THE SKIN

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.780
is directed by Jonathan Glazer.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:29.920
And Jonathan Glazer directed that famous Guinness
horse advertising campaign.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:42.890
I wanted to find an actor that’s quite beautiful
and I wanted them to be together on set during

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:50.700
the office scene because it creates some sort
of duality when opposites interact with each

00:03:50.700 --> 00:04:01.920
other you kind of feel a sense of completion
because a disfigured man with a beautiful

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:08.940
model you kind of feel like they belong to
each other and that’s what we tried to create

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.460
in that specific office scene.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:21.200
Also Fabian Bolin is a method actor, so he
practiced how to actually behave like an animal.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.790
On set we actually called him by his character’s
name rather than his real name.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:32.510
I think method acting is definitely important
when you actually want to embody a character

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:34.530
in a film.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:41.000
Another interesting thing is we picked Fabian
Bolin as the main actor.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:49.900
He is Swedish and Brazilian and we picked
him out of 200 applicants.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:55.030
Swedish people tend to have that sort of villain
type of look.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:05:04.620
And we can see it Bill Skarsgård and we can
see it in that CTO, the guy in MR.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.700
ROBOT.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:11.870
But Swedish people really the villains that
I like.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:19.850
Well, if you want to check the film out it’s
in the link in the description.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:30.780
I’m still connected to companies especially
3-D animation and I’m also really into creating

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:31.780
music videos.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:39.450
If you are a musician or artist in Scandinavia
or anywhere in Europe I would love to work

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:40.450
with you.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:46.340
And I’ve filmed 5 music videos in Iceland
and I really enjoy it.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.340
“What the f?

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:47.343
What the f?”

